        "action": "fill",
        "value": "I'm a Johns Hopkins undergraduate studying Applied Mathematics & Statistics and Economics, and most of what I've built sits close to what your team does: systems that have to keep working when they touch real data and real consequences. At Summer Atlantic Capital / SAC Nexus I build the machine-learning core of an anomaly-detection and alert-prioritization system. I own the event-ingestion pipeline, the repository and CI scaffold, and a versioned FastAPI microservice serving the detection API, plus temporal sequence modeling over event streams and a learning-to-rank layer. What I care about there is operational usefulness, not just model metrics. An anomaly system that fires too many alerts creates a second problem instead of solving the first, so prioritization, failure modes, and documentation matter as much as the model.\n\nThe other thread is knowing where automation should stop. In a Playwright job-application agent I wrote in TypeScript, I used SQLite as the single source of truth because browser automation gets dangerous once state diverges across processes, put every mutating capability behind fail-closed flags, and made unreliable ATS platforms skip rather than guess. Production trading infrastructure seems like the same instinct at a much higher stake, and that's the part I'd want to work on.",
